ERROR REPORTS

Please send errors and suggestions to Frans Kaashoek and Robert Morris
(kaashoek,rtm@mit.edu).  The main purpose of xv6 is as a teaching
operating system for MIT's 6.1810, so we are more interested in
simplifications and clarifications than new features.

BUILDING AND RUNNING XV6

You will need a RISC-V "newlib" tool chain from
https://github.com/riscv/riscv-gnu-toolchain, and qemu compiled for
riscv64-softmmu.  Once they are installed, and in your shell
search path, you can run "make qemu".


LOADABLE KERNEL MODULE DEMO
============================

This xv6 project includes a restricted runtime ELF64 RISC-V ET_REL loader and
the `hello`, `sysmon`, `procmon`, and `klogger` modules. Module execution
is supported only with one QEMU hart. Modules execute as privileged kernel code;
they are not sandboxed.

Build and boot the production module configuration:

    make -B MODULES=1 CPUS=1 kernel/kernel fs.img
    make MODULES=1 CPUS=1 qemu

At the xv6 shell, try:

    modctl load sysmon.o
    modctl run sysmon
    modctl load procmon.o
    modctl run procmon
    modctl load klogger.o
    modctl run klogger add WARN example-entry
    modctl run klogger show
    modctl run klogger clear
    modctl list
    modctl unload sysmon
    modctl unload procmon
    modctl unload klogger

The module objects are separate files installed in `fs.img`; they are not
linked into `kernel/kernel`. `modctl` supports `load file.o`, `run name
[args...]`, `list`, and `unload name`.

To run the one-hart integration suite, build its test-only lifecycle fixtures
and run the harness:

    make -B MODULES=1 MODULE_TESTS=1 CPUS=1 kernel/kernel fs.img
    python3 experiments/run_module_qemu_tests.py

The host ELF validation suite is:

    experiments/run_elfmod_tests.sh
    HOST_SANITIZE=1 experiments/run_elfmod_tests.sh

The loader does not support SMP, asynchronous callbacks/workers, dependencies,
arbitrary kernel symbol imports, or untrusted modules. Use `CPUS=1` for every
module-enabled build and demonstration.
